-- title:
--   Uniform bound on the number of lattice points in a ball
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d$ be the integer lattice inside Euclidean $\mathbb{R}^d$. For **every** centre $a \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and **every** real radius $M$,
--
--   $$\#\bigl(\mathbb{Z}^d \cap \overline{B}(a, M)\bigr) \;\le\; \bigl(2\lceil M \rceil + 1\bigr)^{d},$$
--
--   where $\overline{B}(a,M) = \{x : \lVert x - a\rVert \le M\}$ is the closed Euclidean ball and $\lceil \cdot \rceil$ is the ceiling, truncated at $0$ for negative arguments. No hypothesis at all is placed on $a$ or on $M$: the radius is allowed to be negative or zero, in which case the ball is empty or a point and the inequality is trivial.
--
--   The point of the statement is the uniformity in the centre — the bound depends only on the dimension and the radius, never on where the ball sits — which is exactly what is needed when the same counting estimate must be applied simultaneously at every centre of a lattice of centres.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The cardinality is `Set.encard`, valued in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$, so the statement also records that the intersection is finite. The ceiling is `Nat.ceil`, valued in $\mathbb{N}$; the whole right-hand side is formed in $\mathbb{N}$ and then coerced into $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$.
